22:19:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a fantastic question. It feels illogical, doesn't it? If we have a shiny new, effectively infinite solution (IPv6), why are we still duct-taping the old one (IPv4) together? The reason is that the transition to IPv6 is not a "switch" you can flip; it is a massive, generational infrastructure project. Here is why the "master plan" is taking so long to execute. ### 1. They are two different "languages" The biggest hurdle is that **IPv4 and IPv6 are not backward compatible.** Imagine if the entire world spoke English, and we decided to switch to a new language called "Super-English." You can’t just stop speaking English one day, because then you couldn't talk to anyone who hasn't learned the new language yet. To make them work together, you have to run **"Dual Stack."** This means every router, server, and device has to speak *both* IPv4 and IPv6 at the same time. This is technically difficult, requires more processing power, and is expensive to manage. For many companies, it is cheaper and easier to just keep using IPv4 than to pay to maintain both. ### 2. The "Chicken and Egg" problem This is the classic dilemma that has stalled IPv6 adoption for two decades: * **Content providers** (like Google, Netflix, or your bank) don't want to invest heavily in IPv6 if their users can't connect to it. * **ISPs** don't want to invest heavily in IPv6 if the content providers don't have enough stuff on it yet. * **Users** don't care about IPv6 as long as their internet works. Because nobody wanted to be the first to jump, everyone stayed on IPv4. We are stuck in a loop where IPv4 is still "good enough" for the majority of the internet, so there is very little financial incentive to rush the transition. ### 3. The "If it ain't broke, don't fix it" mentality For a network engineer, changing the fundamental addressing protocol of a massive network is terrifying. It carries a huge risk of breaking things. * **IPv4 is rock solid.** It has been around